= Validation — Technical =

== Purpose-relative adequacy ==
Validation can be represented as an adequacy relation:

<pre>
model/system M
target/use T
acceptance criteria C
evidence E
--------------------------------
E supports adequacy(M,T,C)
</pre>

This makes validation explicitly relative to an intended use and acceptance criteria.

== Model validation ==
Scientific model validation often compares model outputs <code>y_M</code> with experimental/observational data <code>y_D</code> while accounting for:

* measurement uncertainty;
* numerical error;
* parameter uncertainty;
* structural discrepancy;
* input uncertainty.

A small residual does not necessarily imply a correct model if parameters were overfit or measurement error is large.

== Calibration versus validation ==
Calibration estimates model parameters using data.

Validation evaluates model adequacy against relevant evidence, preferably with data or conditions not used to fit the model.

Using the same data for fitting and validation can cause optimistic bias and weak evidence of generalization.

== Predictive validation ==
Predictive-model validation can use:
* held-out test sets;
* cross-validation;
* temporal validation;
* external-site validation;
* calibration curves;
* discrimination metrics;
* proper scoring rules.

Metric choice must follow intended use; no single score proves general validity.

== Construct validation ==
For latent constructs, validation may seek converging and discriminating evidence:
* does the measure correlate with theoretically related variables?
* does it remain distinct from different constructs?
* does the internal structure match the construct model?
* does it behave similarly across relevant groups?

Construct validity is a long-run evidential argument, not a one-time certificate.

== Domain shift ==
A validated system can lose validity under:
* population shift;
* sensor changes;
* policy changes;
* environmental changes;
* changed incentives;
* software updates.

Validation therefore has temporal and environmental scope and may require revalidation.

